The complexity here is in the fact that in

929
00:54:28,989 --> 00:54:31,949
the honest case, any step in the argument
that

930
00:54:31,949 --> 00:54:35,630
is checked will always be correct.
Conversely, in the

931
00:54:35,630 --> 00:54:38,590
dishonest case, any step that is checked
will almost

932
00:54:38,590 --> 00:54:42,070
always be correct, but there will be
occasional strategically

933
00:54:42,150 --> 00:54:44,510
placed errors. So it comes down to how
many

934
00:54:44,510 --> 00:54:47,349
calls to verification of claims we can
make under

935
00:54:47,349 --> 00:54:51,510
constraints of time and computational
resources. To illustrate this,

936
00:54:51,590 --> 00:54:54,900
consider a simplified example which a
large number called

937
00:54:54,900 --> 00:55:00,460
RSA2048. The honest debater shows a
reliable test proving

938
00:55:00,460 --> 00:55:04,659
that RSA2048 can be divided by smaller
prime numbers

939
00:55:04,659 --> 00:55:08,579
and supports this with solid reasoning.
Meanwhile, the dishonest

940
00:55:08,579 --> 00:55:11,099
debater picks a number X and argues there
are

941
00:55:11,099 --> 00:55:13,940
no prime factors smaller than X and no
prime

942
00:55:13,940 --> 00:55:19,059
factors larger than X, concluding
incorrectly that RSA2048 has

943
00:55:19,059 --> 00:55:22,550
no prime factors. Both debaters add more
details to

944
00:55:22,550 --> 00:55:26,349
their arguments. The honest debater's
claims are always accurate,

945
00:55:26,349 --> 00:55:29,349
while the dishonest debater's claims are
usually correct but

946
00:55:29,349 --> 00:55:32,670
occasionally wrong. This makes it hard for
the judge

947
00:55:32,670 --> 00:55:35,269
to tell who is right without checking many
steps

948
00:55:35,269 --> 00:55:37,670
of each argument, which takes a lot of
time

949
00:55:37,670 --> 00:55:38,269
and effort.
